// @test regression/INV175-float-variable-int-param
// @description A float VARIABLE or float EXPRESSION in an `int` param slot is TV's CE10123, exactly like the float literal INV107 pinned - TV narrows strictly under every qualifier (const, simple and series float all rejected). INV107 had scoped the check to a literal and left variables as a follow-up; this widens it. `ta.highest`'s length is checked positionally because every overload types that slot int. An int variable, a literal int, and an explicit int() cast stay clean. Probed 2026-09-07 via pine-lint --tv, see INV175.
// @expects parse: success
// @expects error: line=4, column=19, message=/Cannot call "ta.sma" with argument "length"="lenF"\. An argument of ".* float" type was used but a "series int"/
// @expects error: line=5, column=19, message=/Cannot call "ta.sma" with argument "length"="close"\. An argument of "series float" type/
// @expects error: line=6, column=22, message=/Cannot call "ta.highest" with argument "length"="close"/
// @expects errors: 3

//@version=6
indicator("INV175-float-variable-int-param")
float lenF = 14.0
x = ta.sma(close, lenF)
y = ta.sma(close, close)
z = ta.highest(high, close)
int lenI = 14
ok1 = ta.sma(close, lenI)
ok2 = ta.highest(high, 5)
ok3 = ta.sma(close, int(close))
plot(x + y + z + ok1 + ok2 + ok3)
